// DSB Web Push scheduling Worker. Separate from the site Worker (wrangler.jsonc at the root, name "dsb").
//
// THIS FILE TURNS ON THE SCHEDULE ONLY. IT DOES NOT TURN ON SENDING.
// - triggers.crons is the versioned source of the cron: "25 14,22 * * 1-5"
//   (weekdays, 11:25 and 19:25 Brasília, UTC−3). Same as CRON_DOCUMENTADO.
// - wrangler deploy replaces the account's crons for this Worker with this key. Removing it in a later deploy
//   deletes the schedule. Do not create the cron only in the dashboard.
// - There is no PUSH_SEND var. The cron tick does not send unless the secret is exactly "on"
//   and the SQLite key "envio" is "on" (default: missing = off).
// - Fan-out to the whole KV also needs the SQLite key "fanout" = "on" (default: missing = off).
//   With fanout off, the cron answers fanout_desligado and records no recipients.
// - The VAPID pair, ADMIN_TOKEN and ALERTA_TOKEN are secrets. No value lives in this file.
// - POST /admin/alerta (ALERTA_TOKEN): free text only to Marcelo's fixed allowlist of 2 subscriptions
//   (src/alerta.js). No fan-out, no KV writes or deletes, no change to the briefings' dedupe. (Retired after the
//   dsb-api push cutover, X-31.)
//
// OPERATIONAL SOURCE OF TRUTH
// 1. Sending: SQLite key `envio` (admin /admin/envio). Deploying this file does NOT clear it.
//    The PUSH_SEND secret is the second lock and is not removed by a deploy either (it is not in vars).
//    Until PUSH_SEND is set, the code reads the old secret PUSH_ENVIO (same meaning), so renaming changes nothing.
//    Fan-out: SQLite key `fanout` (admin /admin/fanout). Also survives deploys.
//    All three are independent of the cron.
// 2. Cron: the triggers key below. Do not remove it unless the schedule should stop.
// 3. Never put PUSH_SEND in "vars". A committed "off" would apply on every deploy and would erase a value set
//    as a secret outside the file, with nothing in the diff if someone just "cleans up" the line.
//    Missing + off by default in the code is the safe behaviour.
// 4. Migration v1 creates the SQLite class once. Do not change its tag or delete the class (or the DO instance
//    name "agenda" in src/index.js): that would create a new object and lose the dedupe (risk of resending).
//
// KV DSB_PUSH is the existing namespace of the subscriptions. Do not create another.
// Do not point at Boa Nova do Dia resources. The two products keep separate keys and namespaces.
// Traces stay off: the fetch span records url.full (push endpoint) and the KV span records the key.
{
  "$schema": "../../node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "dsb-push",
  "main": "src/index.js",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-11-07",
  "workers_dev": true,
  // workers.dev is the API host (push-evento uses it); preview URLs are not needed.
  "preview_urls": false,
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true,
    "logs": {
      "enabled": true,
      "head_sampling_rate": 1
    },
    "traces": {
      "enabled": false,
      "head_sampling_rate": 0
    }
  },
  "kv_namespaces": [
    {
      "binding": "DSB_PUSH",
      "id": "1467af16ba4f4d3db8f3c6dd292f2d0b"
    }
  ],
  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [
      {
        "name": "SCHEDULE",
        "class_name": "AgendaPush"
      }
    ]
  },
  "migrations": [
    {
      "tag": "v1-agenda-push",
      "new_sqlite_classes": ["AgendaPush"]
    }
  ],
  "triggers": {
    "crons": ["25 14,22 * * 1-5"]
  }
}
